{
  "dom_id": "step-q213272",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Let $A={Z,Q,R}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,Y⊆X}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14120255/assets/983be4b904bbbf72f896c2e96d3691c74179c9e078494d581fb8c9b2c0c64b7f.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $Y⊂X.$\n\n- If $X=Z,$ then the value $Y=Z$ satisfies $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(Z,Z),$ and we should add the following arrow  to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nZ\\to Z\n$$\n\n- If $X=Q,$ then the values $Y=Z,Q$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(Q,Z),(Q,Q),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nQ\\to Z,\\,Q\\to Q\n$$\n\n- If $X=R,$ then the values $y=Z,Q,R$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(R,Z),$ $(R,Q),$ $(R,R),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nR\\to Z,\\,R\\to Q,\\,R\\to R\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14120255/assets/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
